      "math_academy_id": "e-4105",
      "problem": "Given that $e^{x+1} + 1 = 3,$ what is the value of $x$?",
      "worked_solution": "First, we isolate the exponential, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+1}+1 & =3 \\\\ {e}^{x+1} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the natural logarithm $\\left(\\ln\\right)$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+1} & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x+1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ x+1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x =\\ln\\left(2\\right) -1.$",
